from ot1 import s
s('jericho','The Walls of Jericho','OT',['Joshua 5:13-6:27'],'🎺',
 "God's people came to a big city with high walls. God told Joshua, 'March around the city for six days. On the seventh day, shout!' The people did just what God said. They walked and walked. On the seventh day the priests blew the trumpets, the people shouted, and the walls fell down! God had kept his promise.",
 "After Moses, Joshua led God's people into the promised land. The first city they met was Jericho, with huge walls and its gates shut tight. God gave Joshua a very odd plan: march around the city once a day for six days, with priests carrying the ark and blowing trumpets, and say nothing. On the seventh day they marched around seven times. Then the priests blew a long blast, the people gave a great shout, and the walls collapsed. The people walked in. God had given them the city, and he did it his way, so they would know the victory was his.",
 "Jericho is about trusting God's way even when it seems strange. Joshua is not told to build siege engines. He is told to march, to carry the ark of God's presence and to listen. The story begins with an encounter: a commander of the Lord's army appears and says, 'Take off your sandals, the place where you stand is holy', echoing Moses at the bush. It also includes Rahab, who protected Israel's spies and was saved; she became an ancestor of Jesus. The story is also hard, since it involves war. The Church reads it carefully, remembering that the real victory is always God's mercy over sin.",
 ['Why do you think God gave such an unusual plan?','What does Rahab teach us about trust?','What would it be like to walk and not say a word?'],'Joshua 6:16',hue=40)

s('ruth','Ruth Stays Loyal','OT',['Ruth 1:1-4:22'],'🌾',
 "Ruth loved her mother-in-law Naomi. Naomi was sad and had to go home to Bethlehem. Ruth said, 'I will not leave you. Your people will be my people, and your God will be my God.' In Bethlehem Ruth picked up grain left in the field. A kind man named Boaz helped her. In time Ruth became part of King David's family.",
 "A famine drove Naomi and her family from Bethlehem to the land of Moab. There her husband and sons died, leaving her with her daughters-in-law, Orpah and Ruth. Naomi decided to go back home and told them to stay with their own families. Orpah kissed her and went. But Ruth held on: 'Where you go I will go, where you live I will live. Your people will be my people and your God my God.' In Bethlehem, Ruth gathered leftover barley in the field of Boaz, a kind relative of Naomi. Boaz noticed her goodness and cared for her. In time they married, and their great-grandson was King David.",
 "Ruth is a short book about 'hesed', loving loyalty. Ruth is a foreigner, a widow and poor, yet she chooses to bind herself to Naomi and to Naomi's God. The story shows God's quiet providence in ordinary events: a field, a harvest, a kind man. Boaz acts as 'redeemer', the relative who protects those in need, which is how Christians have understood Christ. And Ruth, from Moab, becomes the great-grandmother of David and so an ancestor of Jesus, named in Matthew's genealogy. God's family is wider than anyone expected.",
 ['What did Ruth give up to stay with Naomi?','How did Boaz show kindness?','Who needs your loyalty and friendship?'],'Ruth 1:16',hue=45)

s('samuel','God Calls Samuel','OT',['1 Samuel 3:1-21'],'🕯️',
 "Samuel was a boy who helped in God's house. One night he heard a voice, 'Samuel!' He ran to Eli. 'Here I am. You called me.' Eli said, 'I did not call. Go back to bed.' This happened again. Then Eli said, 'It is God. Say, Speak, Lord, your servant is listening.' Samuel did, and God spoke to him.",
 "Samuel lived and worked in the house of the Lord with the old priest Eli. In those days, God did not often speak to his people. One night, Samuel heard a voice call, 'Samuel!' He ran to Eli. 'Here I am; you called me.' 'I did not call,' said Eli. 'Go back and lie down.' It happened a second time, and a third. Then Eli understood that God was calling the boy. 'Go and lie down, and if he calls again, say, Speak, Lord, for your servant is listening.' Samuel did, and God gave him a message. Samuel grew up to be a great prophet, and all Israel knew the Lord was with him.",
 "God chooses a child to hear his voice at a time when 'the word of the Lord was rare'. Samuel does not recognise God at first, and it takes the patience of old Eli to help him see. Three times God calls him by name: God knows each of us by name. The answer Eli teaches him, 'Speak, Lord, for your servant is listening', has become a prayer for many. The message to Samuel is hard (Eli's household will fall), and Samuel is afraid to tell it, but he tells the truth. Listening for God includes courage and honesty.",
 ['Why did Samuel think it was Eli who called him?','How can we listen for God?','What prayer could you pray like Samuel?'],'1 Samuel 3:10',hue=235)

s('david-goliath','David and Goliath','OT',['1 Samuel 17:1-58'],'🪨',
 "A giant named Goliath scared the whole army. But young David was not afraid. 'God will help me,' he said. David picked up five smooth stones and his sling. He ran toward Goliath. He threw one stone, and Goliath fell down! God helped David win. David knew it was God's help, not his own strength.",
 "The Philistine army had a champion, a giant named Goliath, over nine feet tall. Every day he shouted at Israel's army, and no one dared to fight him. David, a young shepherd, came to bring food to his brothers. He heard Goliath and said, 'Who is this man, to insult the armies of the living God?' King Saul gave David his armour, but it was too big. David took his shepherd's staff, five smooth stones and a sling. 'You come with a sword and spear,' he said to Goliath, 'but I come in the name of the Lord.' David slung a stone, and it struck Goliath, who fell down. The Philistines ran away.",
 "Everyone expects strength to win, but this story shows trust in God defeating fear. David knows what to do because he has protected his flock from lions and bears, and he sees that he is not alone: 'The battle is the Lord's.' Saul's armour does not fit; David must fight as himself. The story is about courage, but also humility: David credits God, not himself. Many have seen in David a sign of Christ, the shepherd who faces the enemy for the sake of the people and wins what they cannot.",
 ['Who or what is the “Goliath” you are afraid of?','Why did David refuse Saul’s armour?','What did David trust in?'],'1 Samuel 17:47',hue=130)

s('psalm23','The Lord Is My Shepherd','OT',['Psalm 23:1-6'],'🐑',
 "David looked after sheep, and he knew what a good shepherd does. He wrote a song: 'The Lord is my shepherd. I have everything I need. He leads me beside quiet water. Even in a dark valley, I am not afraid, for you are with me.' God looks after us like a shepherd looks after his sheep.",
 "Before David was king he was a shepherd, and he knew how a shepherd cares for his sheep: finding green grass and still water, guiding them on safe paths, and protecting them from wolves with his rod and staff. In Psalm 23, David says God is like that: 'The Lord is my shepherd, I shall not want. Even in the darkest valley I will fear no evil, for you are with me.' God sets a table for us and fills our cup to overflowing. 'Goodness and mercy will follow me all the days of my life.' Jesus later said, 'I am the Good Shepherd.'",
 "Psalm 23 is the most loved psalm in the world, and it is a good one to learn by heart. It begins with trust, 'I shall not want', and moves through quiet pastures, right paths and the valley of the shadow of death, where the psalm turns from 'he' to 'you': in the dark, God is not spoken about but spoken to. The rod and staff are for protecting the sheep. The banquet table and the overflowing cup have made Christians think of the Eucharist, and the 'house of the Lord' of heaven. Jesus applies this image to himself in John 10: the Good Shepherd who lays down his life for the sheep.",
 ['What does a shepherd do for the sheep?','When have you felt God was with you in a dark valley?','What does it mean that goodness and mercy will follow us?'],'Psalm 23:1',hue=125)

s('solomon','Solomon Asks for Wisdom','OT',['1 Kings 3:3-28'],'👑',
 "Solomon became king when he was young. In a dream God said, 'Ask for anything you want.' Solomon did not ask for money or a long life. He said, 'Please give me a wise and listening heart.' God was very pleased. God gave him wisdom, and many good things besides.",
 "When David died, his son Solomon became king. One night God appeared to him in a dream and said, 'Ask what I should give you.' Solomon said, 'I am only a young man, and I do not know how to rule. Give me a listening heart so that I can tell right from wrong.' God was pleased that he had not asked for riches or fame. 'I will give you a wise heart, and I will also give you what you did not ask for.' Soon after, two women came to Solomon, both claiming the same baby. Solomon said, 'Cut the baby in two.' One woman cried, 'No! Give her the baby!' Solomon knew she was the true mother.",
 "Solomon's request is what the Bible calls 'a listening heart' (in Hebrew, one that hears). He knows his weakness and asks for what he needs to serve God's people, not for himself. The test of the two women shows wisdom as the ability to see the heart of a matter. Later Solomon builds the Temple in Jerusalem. The story is also a warning: Solomon's wisdom grows weak when he turns from God. Jesus says that 'something greater than Solomon is here'. The Church has always prayed for the gift of wisdom as one of the seven gifts of the Holy Spirit.",
 ['What would you have asked for?','How did Solomon know who the real mother was?','How can we ask God for wisdom?'],'1 Kings 3:9',hue=48)

s('elijah','Elijah and the Quiet Voice','OT',['1 Kings 17:1-16','1 Kings 19:1-18'],'🐦',
 "Elijah was a prophet who told people to follow God. God took care of him. Birds brought him bread and meat. Later Elijah felt scared and hid in a cave. There was a big wind, an earthquake and a fire. But God was not in those. Then there was a quiet, gentle whisper. God was there, speaking softly to Elijah.",
 "Elijah was a prophet in a time when the king and queen worshipped a false god, Baal. Elijah told them there would be no rain until God said so. God told Elijah to hide by a stream, and ravens brought him bread and meat each morning and evening. Later, a poor widow shared the last of her flour and oil with him, and God made the jar never run out. At last, after a great showdown on Mount Carmel, Elijah ran away in fear and wanted to give up. God fed him through an angel, and Elijah walked forty days to Mount Horeb. There came a great wind, an earthquake and a fire, but the Lord was not in them. Then came a gentle whisper, and Elijah hid his face.",
 "Elijah stands for the courage to tell the truth to power, but this story is about what happens afterwards: he is exhausted and afraid, and asks to die. God's response is gentle: sleep, bread and water, an angel's touch. On the mountain where Moses met God, Elijah expects the Lord in spectacular signs, and finds him in 'a sound of sheer silence'. It is a reminder to make room for silence in prayer. God also corrects Elijah's loneliness by telling him there are seven thousand faithful people and gives him Elisha as a companion. At the Transfiguration, Elijah appears with Moses beside Jesus.",
 ['How did God take care of Elijah?','Where do you find quiet to listen for God?','Why was God not in the wind, earthquake or fire?'],'1 Kings 19:12',hue=20)

s('jonah','Jonah and the Big Fish','OT',['Jonah 1:1-4:11'],'🐟',
 "God said to Jonah, 'Go to the city of Nineveh and tell the people to stop doing wrong.' Jonah did not want to go. He hopped on a boat to go far away! A big storm came. Jonah was thrown into the sea, and a great fish swallowed him! Jonah prayed inside the fish for three days. God saved him. Jonah went to Nineveh. The people said sorry, and God forgave them.",
 "God told Jonah to go to Nineveh, a great city, and warn the people to turn away from their evil. Jonah did not want to, because the people of Nineveh were enemies of Israel. He boarded a ship heading the opposite way. God sent a huge storm. The sailors were terrified, and Jonah said, 'Throw me into the sea, and it will become calm.' They did, and the sea went quiet. God sent a great fish that swallowed Jonah. For three days and nights Jonah prayed from inside the fish, and then the fish spat him onto dry land. This time Jonah went to Nineveh and preached, and everyone, even the king, said sorry. God forgave them. Jonah was cross about it, but God said, 'Should I not care about this great city?'",
 "Jonah is a story with a surprising point. The prophet who runs from God is more stubborn than the sailors or the pagan city. The sailors turn to God; the Ninevites repent after one short sermon; only Jonah is angry that God is merciful. The book ends with God's question to him and to us: should I not pity them? Jesus points to the 'sign of Jonah': three days in the fish, and the Church sees in it a sign of his three days in the tomb and rising again. Mercy is meant to reach everyone, even our enemies.",
 ['Why did Jonah run away?','Why was Jonah angry when God forgave Nineveh?','Who is hard for you to forgive or to care about?'],'Jonah 4:11',hue=195)

s('daniel','Daniel in the Lions’ Den','OT',['Daniel 6:1-28'],'🦁',
 "Daniel prayed to God three times every day. Some men made a law: nobody could pray to anyone except the king. But Daniel still prayed to God. The king had to put Daniel in a pit with lions. All night God sent an angel who closed the lions' mouths. In the morning Daniel was safe! The king said, 'Daniel's God is the living God!'",
 "Daniel served under King Darius in Babylon and was so honest and wise that the king planned to put him in charge of the whole kingdom. Jealous officials tricked the king into signing a law: for thirty days, anyone who prayed to a god or man other than the king would be thrown into the lions' den. Daniel heard about the law, but he went home, opened his windows towards Jerusalem and prayed three times a day, as he always did. He was arrested, and the king, who liked Daniel, was very sad. 'May your God rescue you,' he said. At dawn, the king hurried to the den. 'Daniel! Has your God saved you?' 'My God sent his angel and shut the lions' mouths,' Daniel called.",
 "Daniel lives in exile, in the court of a foreign king, and he does not hide his faith. When a law forbids prayer, he does the simplest, bravest thing: he keeps praying, in the open, just as he did before. His faithfulness is not stubbornness; he does not lead a revolt or insult the king. The king himself ends the story by praising Daniel's God. Early Christians saw in Daniel a picture of martyrs, and in the sealed den a sign of Christ's tomb, from which he came out alive.",
 ['Why did Daniel keep praying?','What could happen if we hide our faith?','What is your daily habit of prayer?'],'Daniel 6:26',hue=38)

s('esther','Queen Esther','OT',['Esther 3:1-9:32'],'👸',
 "Esther became queen. A wicked man planned to hurt her people, the Jews. Her cousin Mordecai said, 'Maybe you are queen for a time like this.' Esther was afraid, but she prayed and was brave. She told the king. The king stopped the plan, and God's people were saved.",
 "Esther was a Jewish girl who became queen of Persia, though no one knew she was Jewish. A wicked official, Haman, tricked the king into signing a law to destroy all the Jews. Her cousin Mordecai begged Esther to go to the king. It was dangerous, because anyone who came to the king without being called could be put to death. Esther asked her people to fast and pray for three days, and then said, 'If I perish, I perish.' She went in, and the king held out his sceptre to her. At two banquets she told him the truth. The king stopped Haman's plot, and the Jews were saved. The feast of Purim remembers it.",
 "In Esther's story God's name is never mentioned, yet his hand is felt everywhere. A hidden queen, an unlikely moment and a few brave words change history. Mordecai's question, 'Who knows whether you have come to the kingdom for such a time as this?', is for every one of us. Esther does not act alone: she asks the community to pray and fast first. In the Catholic Bible Esther's own prayer is included, 'Lord, help me, who am alone and have no helper but you.' She is a model of courage that depends on God.",
 ['What made Esther brave?','How did prayer and fasting help Esther?','What might God be asking you to do “for such a time as this”?'],'Esther 4:14',hue=300)

s('tobit','Tobias and the Angel','OT',['Tobit 5:1-12:22'],'🧭',
 "Tobias had to go on a long trip. His father said, 'Find a guide to go with you.' A kind man named Azarias joined him. Azarias helped Tobias on the road. At the end, he said, 'I am the angel Raphael. God sent me to help you.' The angel Raphael means 'God heals'. God sends angels to look after us.",
 "Old Tobit was blind and poor, though he had always been generous and good. He sent his son Tobias on a long journey to collect some money. Tobias's mother worried, so Tobias went with a guide, Azarias, and his little dog. On the way, a big fish leapt out of the river. Azarias told Tobias to keep the fish's heart, liver and gall, which would be useful as medicine. At the end of the journey, Tobias used the medicine to heal his father's eyes and to help a young woman named Sarah. Then Azarias said, 'I am Raphael, one of the seven angels who stand before God.' Raphael means 'God heals'.",
 "The Book of Tobit is full of good humour and warmth, and it is only in Catholic and Orthodox Bibles. Tobit buries the dead and gives to the poor even when it costs him; young Sarah prays through her sorrow; and God hears both prayers, sending the angel Raphael, who walks with Tobias as a friend. Raphael is one of the three archangels the Church honours. The story reminds us that God cares about family, travel, courtship and the small journeys of life, and that angels are real helpers, though we usually do not recognise them until later.",
 ['How did Raphael help Tobias without being noticed?','What does the name Raphael mean?','How do angels help us today? Do you pray to your guardian angel?'],'Tobit 12:15',hue=210)
